ehind is safest**.\n\nCould you bluff? Yes, but opponent may have checked a medium-strength hand (like middle pair) and fold to a bet. But why risk it when you might have the best hand?\n\nActually, many weak hands (like A-high, K-high) may call a small bet.\n\nBut rules say minimum raise is 100 (or more). That’s a relatively large bet into a pot that likely stayed small (all checks).\n\nAssuming blinds were 50/100, and no bets, pot is 100 (small blind + big blind). Then all checks → pot still 100.\n\nA 100-chip bet is **pot-sized** — very large for a bluff or value bet with one pair.\n\nUnlikely to get called by worse, and likely to get called by better.\n\nTherefore, **checking is optimal**.\n\nReason:  \nThe opponent has shown consistent passivity by checking all rounds, indicating a weak or marginal hand. My hand (pair of 9s with Ace kicker) is likely the best hand at showdown. Betting risks losing additional chips to stronger hands while unlikely to extract value from worse hands. Checking ensures a showdown without further risk.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
